  {
    "subject": "eq",
    "lm_index": 0,
    "topics": [
      {
        "title": "What is an equity instrument?",
        "body": {
          "schema": "notes/v1",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "inline": [
                {
                  "t": "An equity instrument is a security that represents part ownership of a company. The owner holds a fractional claim on assets, but only on what remains after every higher-priority claim is settled."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                [
                  {
                    "t": "Loss is capped at the amount invested. This is "
                  },
                  {
                    "t": "limited liability",
                    "b": true
                  },
                  {
                    "t": "."
                  }
                ],
                [
                  {
                    "t": "Ownership may carry voting rights over company policy."
                  }
                ],
                [
                  {
                    "t": "Returns come from dividends and share price changes."
                  }
                ]
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "level": 3,
              "text": "The liquidation waterfall"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "inline": [
                {
                  "t": "When a company is wound up, cash is distributed in strict priority order."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "infographic",
              "kind": "waterfall",
              "data": [
                {
                  "label": "1st · Debt",
                  "text": "Debt holders: principal and interest",
                  "pct": 100
                },
                {
                  "label": "2nd · Preferred",
                  "text": "Preferred shareholders",
                  "pct": 76
                },
                {
                  "label": "3rd · Common",
                  "text": "Common: the rest",
                  "pct": 52
                }
              ],
              "alt": "Priority of claims: 1st · Debt, then 2nd · Preferred, then 3rd · Common"
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "variant": "example",
              "title": "Worked example",
              "inline": [
                {
                  "t": "Aditya Foods is liquidated and raises ₹90 crore. Debt claims are ₹70 crore and preference shares carry a ₹15 crore liquidation preference. Left after debt: 90 − 70 = ₹20 crore. After preference: 20 − 15 = "
                },
                {
                  "t": "₹5 crore for common shareholders",
                  "b": true
                },
                {
                  "t": "."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "variant": "watch_out",
              "title": "Watch out",
              "inline": [
                {
                  "t": "Preferred shares rank above common but below debt. Do not call preferred shares \"debt\" because the dividend is not a legal obligation."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "variant": "fun",
              "title": "Funny use case",
              "inline": [
                {
                  "t": "Think of a wedding buffet. Debt holders are the family elders served first, preferred shareholders are the cousins, and common shareholders queue at the end hoping the gulab jamun has not run out."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "title": "Common and preferred shares",
        "body": {
          "schema": "notes/v1",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "inline": [
                {
                  "t": "Preferred shares sit between debt and common equity. They usually pay a fixed dividend and carry limited or no voting rights."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "header": [
                "Feature",
                "What it means",
                "Who benefits"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "Cumulative",
                  "Missed dividends build up as arrears",
                  "Holder"
                ],
                [
                  "Non-cumulative",
                  "Missed dividends are lost",
                  "Issuer"
                ],
                [
                  "Participating",
                  "Extra dividend if profits exceed a threshold",
                  "Holder"
                ],
                [
                  "Convertible",
                  "Can switch into common shares",
                  "Holder"
                ],
                [
                  "Callable",
                  "Issuer may redeem at a set price",
                  "Issuer"
                ],
                [
                  "Putable",
                  "Holder may sell back at a set price",
                  "Holder"
                ]
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "variant": "formula",
              "title": "Formula",
              "inline": [
                {
                  "t": "Annual preference dividend = dividend rate × par value × number of shares"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "variant": "example",
              "title": "Worked example",
              "inline": [
                {
                  "t": "Sanvi Steels has 20,000 cumulative 9% preference shares of ₹100 par. Nothing was paid for 2 years. Yearly dividend = 9% × 100 × 20,000 = ₹1,80,000. Arrears plus this year = 3 × 1,80,000 = "
                },
                {
                  "t": "₹5,40,000",
                  "b": true
                },
                {
                  "t": " due before any common dividend."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "variant": "watch_out",
              "title": "Watch out",
              "inline": [
                {
                  "t": "Arrears include the current year only if the current dividend is also being declared. Read whether the question asks for arrears alone or the total."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      },
